    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.5194/egusphere-2026-377",
    "abstract": "Abstract. Predicting dune mobility under changing climatic conditions remains a challenge in aeolian geomorphology, particularly in data-scarce regions. This study presents a novel application of binomial logistic regression to forecast dune activation and migration using readily available meteorological data. We combine established dune mobility indices (Tsoar and Lancaster) into a new integrated index (TsoLa) and evaluate its performance against observed dune migration rates derived from satellite imagery. The model incorporates wind speed, precipitation, and the Southern Annular Mode (SAM) as predictors, achieving robust predictive accuracy (AUC &gt; 0.75) for two distinct coastal dune fields in NE Patagonia, Argentina. Our results demonstrate that even with standard climatic inputs, logistic regression can effectively identify periods of dune activity, offering a low-cost tool for coastal management. The approach is transferable to other aeolian systems, providing a framework for assessing dune dynamics under current and future climate scenarios.",
    "field": "Forecasting coastal dune mobility",
    "problem": "Traditional descriptive methods for assessing coastal dune mobility, such as mobility indices, offer limited predictive capacity for forecasting dune activation or stabilization over time, representing a significant methodological gap, particularly in data-scarce regions where complex numerical simulations are not feasible.",
    "method": "The authors propose a binomial logistic regression model driven by meteorological data and climate indices to forecast the probability of coastal dune activation versus stabilization.",
    "task": "The task is to develop and validate a binomial logistic regression model to forecast coastal dune mobility in northeastern Patagonia, Argentina, using readily available meteorological data and climate indices as predictors.",
